Z||000005||Motorhead||05-22-99||12:58 PM||||Okay ya'll, here's the scoop on the headlight problem.<p>The left side(operates properly) has a knurled knob on top of the motor assembly.  The right side(only opens fully with hood up, and I just noticed that it raises further than the other and doesn't go down as far as the left) has a smooth knob.  Didn't Pontiac change the designe of these assemblies?  <p>Are they interchangeable?<p>Looks to me like the previous owner of this car has struck again!<p>AAAAAARRRRRGGGGG!!!!!<p>HELP!<p>
Z||000006||theogre||05-22-99||02:29 PM||||they may have gotten a motor off some other GM car. The motors for other cars may function electrically, but the crank arms and other things are often differant.<p>the 87+ motor cannot be pluged into a pre 87 Fiero at all. They can't go the other way either. the 2 designs are completely diferant electrically.
Z||000007||Bozone||05-25-99||06:28 PM||||Yup! That sounds familiar. I'm going through the same thing on my 86Gt. Right side goes down but not up. Hafta crank it up manually. I've been told that the mechanisms from and 87 or 88 are superior to the older ones and have opted to have those installed on my OTHER 86Gt that is in for a minor front end crumple. This requires a rewire also but... <br>But I'm wrestling with the first one and have been for a month. I've replaced the whole assembly, checked all the solenoids, replaced one motor (that got me to a point where it goes down anyway) and I'm tackling it again. What a system!
Z||000008||Bozone||05-25-99||06:31 PM||||Oh ogre, the reason I posted the previous is to say that Yes, you can put 87-88 headlight *assemblies* in an older car but you have to rewire. You're right - I doubt you can just put a newer motor in an older housing with older wiring. 
Z||000009||PaUL||05-25-99||07:29 PM||||About 5 years ago, I fried one of my headlight motors, this one had a knurled knob. I got a brand new replacement from the GM dealer, this one came with a smooth knob. I can only assume that the smooth knobed one that motorhead has is newer. <p>The difference between the old and new designs are in the control mechanism/circuit. The old design has mechanical reed switches that shut it off when fully open or closed. The new design has a electronic control circuit with no mechanical switches, so it should last longer.
